Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
i'm joined now by the shadow health  secretary we're streeting a very happy  new year to you good to see you this  morning thanks for being with us all a  bit worrying this christmas and new year  uh over and as we head into 2022 some  really worrying statistics about just  how many of us are going to be unable to  get into work because we're sick with  covid the sunday times saying up to one  in 10 staff sick in the nhs the male  actually saying one in 40 but whatever  the numbers are  it's a concern what's the answer  it is a concern and happy new year i  think the key challenge for the  government at this moment in time is to  get testing right because if we've got  sufficient testing in place that's a key  tool in  managing the risk of the virus managing  the spread of the virus keeping pupils  learning as the school term starts  keeping nhs staff  safe and other workers in critical  national infrastructure safe  and avoiding the need for further  restrictions particularly in england so  that's the big message for the  government today i am concerned that  we've had shortages of tests the health  secretary told me only three weeks ago  that the availability of tests wasn't a  problem clearly it is  and if we get testing right we can avoid  the need for further restrictions so  that's the big message for the  government today  yeah the government's saying it's  tripling the supply of lateral flow  tests to 300 million  by the end of the month but of course  that is still some way off so you say no  more restrictions what about masks in  secondary schools then um kids going to  have to wear them in the classroom now  well i think in terms of schools if the  choice is between  having masters at schools or children  missing schools in huge numbers  of course we want to keep pupils  learning that's got to be the priority i  think there are two things though that  the government can and should be doing  the first is on testing people should be  testing twice a week there's lots of  evidence to suggest that hasn't been  happening properly so the message for  government is get the tests in place  make sure pupils are able to test twice  a week and my message to parents and  pupils is do take the test because the  big challenge this month is going to be  keeping pupils learning avoiding mass  absences and of course making sure that  staff are well enough to attend school  as well so the testing is crucial the  other thing of course is ventilation  ventilation is a really big tool  in preventing the spread of this virus  it's an airborne virus we've urged the  government to act on testing repeat on  ventilation repeatedly they've made an  announcement today that they're going to  send out 7 000 air purifiers to schools  across the country i mean that's only  one in four schools across england so  nowhere near enough and i'm not clear  from what the government said whether  this is one system per school or one air  purifier per school because there's a  big difference between the two  and again i'm afraid it looks a little  bit like a rushed last minute  announcement to give the appearance of  doing something on a big issue where  they should have taken action a lot  sooner so i think the government's got a  lot more to do on ventilation in schools  as well as a key defense a key  mitigation against the virus  we're in this strange situation at the  moment aren't we where we don't yet know  what the effect of us all being able to  mix at christmas that intergenerational  mixing over christmas and the new year  we don't know what effect that is going  to have on the numbers yet we probably  won't know for a couple of weeks and on  the one hand you've got sajid javid and  indeed labour saying that more  restrictions are absolutely the last  resort but on the other hand you've got  the bma saying the nhs is already  overwhelmed it's in a parlour state and  we need restrictions now um who's right  well we're listening very closely to  what  some of the country's leading scientific  minds are saying i've had lots of  meetings in recent days between  christmas and the new year to get a  sense of of what their view is i'm  listening very closely to what nhs  leaders are saying i mean in the end  this is the key challenge for the  country it's to make sure that the nhs  isn't overwhelmed that it's there for us  when we need it and that's not just  about covid that's about the big  pressures we normally see on the nhs at  this time of year it's also about  critical national infrastructure so i  want to reassure people about a couple  of things first is that in the event  that new measures are needed labor will  do the right thing we're not going to  play party politics on votes we showed  that before christmas  the second is we are urging government  to do everything they can to avoid the  need for further restrictions i think  testing is a really crucial tool in all  of this which is why i'm concerned about  the testing shortages  because in the end i would labor fix  that because we know it's an issue how  would labour have done things  differently than with testing because  we've all been testing like crazy we've  been doing what we were told taking  tests before we meet granny uh or go to  the pub um and uh obviously um  there's a supply issue but how would  labour solve that  what were two things firstly  people have been doing the right thing  you're absolutely right and it shouldn't  have been a surprise to the government  that there's been high demand for  testing because they've encouraged it  and in fact we we introduced as part of  the plan b measures  the covered pass scheme where people can  produce negative tests in order to  access nightclubs and larger venues so  we are actively using testing so we  challenged the government to make sure  there were sufficient tests in place and  were told only three weeks ago that was  the case clearly it isn't and that's not  good enough so there is a question here  about basic competence from the  government of course we are where we are  this isn't where we would have started  as the labour party but we are where we  are and so if prior if if we have a  shortage of tests and priorities have to  be made our priorities are keeping  pupils learning so prioritizing schools  prioritizing nhs staff and critical  national infrastructure that that's got  to be the priority for now but the  government does need to get its act  together on the supply of testing and i  think the health secretary needs to  explain  why it was that only three weeks ago he  told me in the house of commons that  availability of tests wasn't a problem  and yet now it so clearly is  perhaps it was because uh there was this  messaging that more restrictions weren't  going to be brought in but  we all needed to be cautious and it does  appear that we we have listened to that  we have been more cautious and we have  done things uh sensibly and just before  we let you go um  what are your thoughts for the next few  weeks the next few months um the data  does seem to be a little bit confusing  in many ways and you can understand why  people are confused at the moment  because on the one hand we have these  huge number of cases you know well over  150 000 cases  a day and yet on the other hand deaths  thankfully uh don't seem to be  increasing dramatically and also the  number of people on uh ventilators in  icu also seems to be steady and  plateauing um it's very hard for people  as we enter into the new year to know  exactly where we're going  yeah and i think it's important that  politicians whether in government or  opposition  aren't complacent about the need to  continually explain to the public what's  going on what numbers we're watching and  why so that people can understand the  decisions we're taking so on on that  note i would just say to people that the  key challenges we've got to keep an eye  on this month in particular are nhs  staff numbers and there's a sunday times  article suggesting 10 of nhs workers  were knocked out on new year's eve  because that the numbers of staff in the  nhs is going to have a big impact on  their availability to deliver the  service this is a people-based service  ultimately so we've got to keep an eye  on nhs staff numbers we've got to  prioritize schools and making sure that  children can keep on learning and the  third thing we need to keep an eye on is  there are some signs from the data that  there's now an uptick in the numbers of  older people who are now getting the  omicron variant so care homes are going  to be a pressure this month as well so  so all in all the things that we can put  in the positive column is that so far  there are signs that this variant isn't  as harmful as previous variants the  reason we can't be complacent about that  and this is in the challenging column  is the the virus is spreading rapidly it  is knocking out lots of staff in the nhs  and other critical national  infrastructure it is going to be a  challenge for schools this month it is  going to be a challenge for care homes  so the big challenge for 2022 because  the virus isn't going away the big  challenge for 2022 is how we put in  place the infrastructure  to make sure that we can live well with  covid without having to go in and out of  lockdowns or other restrictions like the  hokey cokey i understand why people are  tired of that so that means testing i  think in particular  really good tool  and getting the vaccine roll out  continuing so if you haven't had your  first second or booster jab do it now it  really is the best tool the best defense  we have against this virus okay we're  out of time we appreciate you being with  us this morning we're streeting shadow  health secretary thank you

